  

<h2 id="plus-a-pomodoro-style-foss-app-for-focus-when-you-need-it">Plus a pomodoro style <span class="small-caps">FOSS</span> app for focus when you need it</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/6685b59d-cec2-4e14-90d1-43273272e5a8/pasted-image-20261116062637.png" title="Screenshot with a pomodoro app on the left, the time remaining in white text inside a green ring on a mid grey blue background. A cropped portion of this article set in a monospaced font is showing on the right." alt="Screenshot with a pomodoro app on the left, the time remaining in white text inside a green ring on a mid grey blue background. A cropped portion of this article set in a monospaced font is showing on the right." width="1568" height="1022"></p>
<h3 id="foss-pomodoro-app"><span class="small-caps">FOSS</span> pomodoro app</h3>
<p>Pomodorolm is a free and open source pomodoro-style app, now in process of being renamed to <a href="https://concentre.org" target="_blank"><em>Concentré</em></a>, which is French for <em>focus</em>. Available for Mac, Windows, Linux.</p>
<p>You may find, as I did, that the app refuses to open on a Mac. Thanks to the support documents, I entered <code>sudo xattr -cr /Applications/pomodorolm.app</code> into the Terminal and the problem was solved.</p>
<p>I like how Concentré stays on top of the window I’m working in. It has some useful controls. You can set a certain number of rounds for a work session and a maximum session length, with your choice of the length of sub-session and breaks, and the ability to skip rounds. It also has configurable themes and sound effects.</p>
<p>I’d like to see a menu bar version.</p>
<h3 id="obsidian-now-moves-task-lines-with-the-nested-lines-below-it">Obsidian now moves task lines with the nested lines below it</h3>
<p>Obsidian’s recent update to smart lists has made my plain text calendar and task lists easier to use. Moving a line up and down now takes nested items with it.</p>
<p>Previously, using the line-up and line-down commands was literal: only the active line would be moved. This broke parent-child relationships in outlines, requiring you to select all the lines you wanted to move, first. The new behaviour is coming in handy for moving nested events between days in my <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/10/26/ptpl-230-paper-rulers-and-text-calendars" class="wikilink">plain text calendar</a>!</p>
<p>You can find it in <code>Settings &gt; Editor &gt; Smart Lists</code>. My only wish is for a hotkey toggle to turn it on and off. There isn’t one, so another option is to install the Line Mover plugin and set hotkeys for moving single lines.</p>
<p>I’ve only just discovered Line Mover. So far it is working well, though it does not work the same way as the built-in line-up and line-down commands. Trying it on text (like this) just shifts the cursor from the current position to the start or end of the paragraph. I’ll be sticking with my <span class="small-caps">⌘1</span> and <span class="small-caps">⌘2</span> hotkeys for moving lines up and down with their nested items, and save the Line Mover hotkeys for single lines.</p>
